 "id": "2026-11-05-georgia-tennessee-record-flood",
 "lang": "en",
 "script": [
  {
   "id": "hook",
   "text": "A Georgia river just broke its 1990 record. Where did the water come from?"
  },
  {
   "id": "f1",
   "text": "Overnight, the Toccoa River crested at eighteen feet at Copperhill, Tennessee, a record, gauge readings show. The old mark was seventeen point seven five feet, from 1990. The National Weather Service issued a flash flood emergency, a rare warning for catastrophic, life-threatening flooding."
  },
  {
   "id": "f2",
   "text": "The Associated Press reports three to five inches of rain fell overnight in the mountains. The Tennessee Valley Authority reported eight to ten inches between Blue Ridge and Ellijay. Officials counted dozens of water rescues, at least fifty seven by one local station's count."
  },
  {
   "id": "f3",
   "text": "McCaysville declared a state of emergency. Its water plant shut down, and a boil water advisory followed. The police chief blamed drainage problems near highway construction. Gauge readings stay preliminary until the weather service reviews them."
  },
  {
   "id": "cta",
   "text": "Will another storm flood the South this month? Yes or no?"
  }
 ],
